probator
---------

An HTTP(S) reachability prober that reads subdominer JSONL from stdin and probes
each unique hostname.


Usage

    probator [options] subdominer-output.jsonl


Options

    -workers <count>
        Concurrent hostnames to probe.
        Defaults to 20.

    -dns-timeout <duration>
        DNS lookup timeout.
        Defaults to 5s.

    -timeout <duration>
        HTTP request timeout.
        Defaults to 10s.

    -max-redirects <count>
        Maximum redirects per request.
        Defaults to 5.

    -max-body-size <bytes>
        Maximum response body bytes read.
        Defaults to 1048576.

    -user-agent <value>
        HTTP User-Agent.
        Defaults to reconnoisseur/1.0.

    -log <path>
        Write logs to the specified file.
        Defaults to ./probator.log.

    -silent
        Disable application logging.


Examples

    probator < input.jsonl

    subdominer example.org | probator > output.jsonl


Input

    probator accepts subdominer schema version 1 JSONL records.  It uses only
    schema_version, domain, and url. Other fields, such as evidence, are ignored
    and not retained.

    Hostnames are lowercased and have a trailing DNS root dot removed before
    deduplication.  Each unique normalized hostname is probed once.  Duplicate
    records with conflicting normalized domain values are rejected.

    Malformed records, missing required fields, unsupported schema versions, and
    conflicting domains are fatal input errors.  Blank lines are ignored.


Output

    One JSONL record is emitted per unique hostname, in probe-completion order.
    Each output record retains schema_version, domain, and normalized URL, and
    adds a top-level probator object containing DNS, HTTP, and HTTPS
    observations.  The input evidence field is omitted.

    HTTP status codes are observations, not errors.  Responses such as 401, 403,
    404, and 500 are successful HTTP probes.

    Logs are written to file, separately from the JSONL results, so that stdout
    can be redirected or piped into other tools.
